#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055697} :activity/id #uuid "cade22aa-6331-58b6-892c-984d4c3bae76" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048548 :topic/id #uuid "a8f3106d-906d-52d1-b148-e532348d89d2" :topic/math-academy-id 79} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055697 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055997 :tutorial/content "Polynomials with one, two, or three terms have special names.\n\n- A polynomial with one term is called a **monomial**. For example:\n \n\n$$\n1, \\qquad y,\t\\qquad\t2x^3,\t\\qquad\t5a^{4}\n$$\n\nHowever, the following expressions are not monomials because they can't be called polynomials:\n \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{y},\t\\qquad\t5t^{-1},\t\\qquad \\sqrt{x}, \\qquad \t2z^{1/4}\n$$\n\n- A polynomial with two terms is called a **binomial**. For example:\n\n$$\nx+3,\t\\qquad\t7n^2 +n,\t\\qquad\tp^{32} -1\n$$\n\n- A polynomial with three terms is called a **trinomial**. For example:\n \n\n$$\nz^2 -z +1,\t\\qquad\tx^3 -2x -1,\t\\qquad\t2c^{4} - 3c^{3} +4c^2\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "5e82b56d-3e2d-582a-b73f-3c5c98830a58" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6660 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "d5ad5bf3-9168-540f-8492-ce7c9fc4b988" :step/math-academy-id 40308 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055698}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055698 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054710 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054348 :question/id #uuid "b8742776-1dc7-5816-afb6-9a72142ecb5c" :question/math-academy-id "e-10650"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d3a71018-8f67-53cb-aa6c-7f17e0af9ef9" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050306}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055300} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055301} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475965} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475967}] :knowledge-point/title "Categorizing a Polynomial by the Number of Terms It Has"} :step/id #uuid "38cccf7d-917a-5a79-aa78-f1a2b54e1a10" :step/math-academy-id 40309 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055699}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055699 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055998 :tutorial/content "Each type of polynomial of degree between $0$ and $5$ has a specific name. These are shown in the table below.\n\n| Degree | Name | Example |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | Constant | $7$ |\n| $1$ | Linear | $3x + 2$ |\n| $2$ | Quadratic | $2x^2 - 6x + 1$ |\n| $3$ | Cubic | $x^3 + 4x^2 + 9x$ |\n| $4$ | Quartic | $3x^4 - 5x^2$ |\n| $5$ | Quintic | $7x^5 + 3x^4 - x^4 + x - 6$ |\n\nWe can combine these with the names we learned earlier. \n\nFor example:\n\n- $2x+1$ is a *linear binomial* because its degree equals $1,$ and it has $2$ terms.\n\n- $x^2$ is a *quadratic monomial* because its degree equals $2,$ and it has $1$ term.\n\n- $2x^3+x+1$ is a *cubic trinomial* because its degree equals $3,$ and it has $3$ terms.\n\nIf a polynomial contains more than three terms, then we refer to it by its degree only.\n\nFor example:\n\n- $x^3+2x^2+x-1$ is a *cubic polynomial.*\n\n- $x^4+x^3+x^2+x+1$ is a *quartic polynomial.*" :tutorial/id #uuid "ed2578f7-b1e4-5f83-b963-d66d7b97fa9a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6661 :tutorial/title "Names of Polynomials Based on Their Degree"} :step/id #uuid "ac189d1f-bdee-52ff-86b2-d371b3d6e1c6" :step/math-academy-id 40312 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055700}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055700 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054711 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054349 :question/id #uuid "6270e77f-6ccb-5304-90da-2419fa309007" :question/math-academy-id "e-10651"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b8feb96-7432-5fe7-a6c7-b2d8ebe7e766" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048406}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055361} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055442} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234398} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475918} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475973}] :knowledge-point/title "Categorizing a Polynomial by Its Degree and Number of Terms"} :step/id #uuid "08e0cbe9-22f1-5b66-a44f-4af1547552f5" :step/math-academy-id 40313 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055701}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055701 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054712 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054350 :question/id #uuid "3400928d-9463-56f2-af47-e193a1e18a47" :question/math-academy-id "e-10652"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0bd2d5a4-73de-51ad-9f9f-070d1d4f31a5" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048524}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055015} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055016} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475925} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475927}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements"} :step/id #uuid "227abef4-c6c1-5c73-9556-5ca01a686b40" :step/math-academy-id 40316}] :activity/title "Monomials, Binomials and Trinomials" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186054924}]}
